  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Three&nbsp;rods of identical cross-section and length are made of three different materials of thermal conductivity&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, respectively. They are joined together at their ends to make a long rod (see figure). One end of&nbsp;the long rod is maintained at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;and the other at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;(see figure). If the joints of the rod are at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>70</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;in steady and there is no loss of energy from the surface of the rod, the correct relationship between&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">,</mo><mo mathvariant=\"italic\">&#160;</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn mathvariant=\"italic\">2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is :</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0064_b3f08d35d944.png\" style=\"width: 589px; height: 145px;\" />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"
  },
